WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.840
I'm exploring the fascinating world of plants from the most bizarre to the most beautiful.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:32.680
With new technology, we can reveal aspects of their lives that are otherwise hidden from us.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:41.480
We can change time to discover a dynamic world of constant motion.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:50.400
We can change dimension to watch them interacting with insects.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:01:00.960
We can analyze how they communicate with color, with scent, even heat.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:09.720
And we can discover how fungi are not the enemies of plants, but their essential partners.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:16.880
And we can watch all these dramas unfolding in just one unique place, outside in the gardens

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:24.240
and inside these spectacular pavilions of glass.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:48.600
The Royal Botanic Gardens of Kew.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.960
Our senses match what is important to us.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.080
Our eyes can detect just tiny little movements and see best during the day.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:04.520
Our ears can detect the frequencies of the human voice.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.200
But there are many things that are important to plants that we can't detect.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:37.120
This is an exploration of that hidden world.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.240
Plants may seem passive and inactive, but in fact they move.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.360
These are among the most mysterious.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.720
Sundews.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.320
The leaves, like those of other plants, use sunlight to help them grow.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.480
But their glistening tentacles get food in another way.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.000
They are traps.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:16.480
Plants to grow properly need sunlight and water and minerals and nutrients which they

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.600
get from the soil.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.460
But in some parts of the world, in swamps and bogs for example, there are very few minerals

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.500
and nutrients in the soil.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.640
So plants have to get those things from somewhere else.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:35.520
And they get them from the bodies of dead animals.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.880
Each tentacle is tipped with a glistening droplet.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:45.480
It may look like nectar and indeed many insects seem especially attracted to it.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.600
But it's not.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:04:01.000
It's a glue.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.840
It not only holds the insect fast, but clogs up the tiny holes on its flanks so that it

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:15.320
can't breathe.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.760
Time lapse cameras reveal that tentacles that have not been touched by the insect now start

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.540
to bend towards it.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:35.040
How they detect the insect's presence we still don't know.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:54.480
Suddenly the sundew begins to fold its whole leaf around its prey.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.660
There will be no escape from this lethal embrace.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:07.600
Now the plant liquefies its victim's internal organs while it's still alive and absorbs

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:21.840
the nutrients through its leaves.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:31.800
But carnivorous plants don't require special photography to show how fast they can be.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:40.680
Darwin described this plant as one of the most wonderful in the world.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:47.640
It's a native of the coastal plains of North and South Carolina in America.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:52.120
The Venus flytrap.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.920
It catches insects with booby traps.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:03.400
At the top the leaves are baited with a sweet nectar.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:09.560
But lower down there are a few upright bristles.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:14.960
Triggers.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:23.920
Many things might accidentally touch one of them so a single touch has no effect.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.040
But a fly crawling around on the leaf sipping the nectar is likely to touch more than one

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.340
of the bristles.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:58.200
But if it touches two within 20 seconds, that's different.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:08.320
It's thought that the trigger hairs work by releasing a rudimentary electric impulse.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.160
The victim's desperate attempts to find a way out simply trigger more hairs and encourage

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:23.240
the trap to close even more tightly.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:34.200
The plant begins to release juices rich in hydrochloric acid.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:45.120
It will take 10 days to completely digest its meal.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.960
But there's a carnivorous plant that moves at even greater speeds.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:08:00.160
It lives in the hidden underwater world of lakes and ponds.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:09.280
It's called eutricularia, bladderwort.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:19.120
These tiny wrigglers are mosquito larvae and they are among its prey.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.740
The bladders that give the plant its name are not floats.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:33.400
They too are traps.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:41.100
Each bladder contains a partial vacuum and has a one-way entrance.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:48.160
By slowing down the action 240 times, we can see how they work.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:57.120
With the slightest touch, the door flies open inwards, sweeping the prey inside.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:10.640
It all happens in less than a millisecond.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:14.600
Eutricularia is the plant world's swiftest killer.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:24.640
But most plants, of course, move very slowly indeed.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:32.880
Our cameras over a year can show just how dramatic such seasonal changes are.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.960
It's winter in Kew.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:43.940
It's so cold and the sun's rays are so feeble that plants can't grow.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.160
But leaves in winter can be a liability.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.720
A fully canopied tree could be uprooted by the winter gales.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:10:00.120
And in any case, the flimsy leaves of oaks and beeches would be destroyed by the frosts.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:08.400
So many such trees stand naked and inactive throughout the winter.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:17.080
But eventually the sun begins to rise higher and higher in the sky, the days get warmer

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:37.360
and spring arrives.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.520
Heat and heat-sensitive molecules inside plant cells are the triggers of germination and

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:57.200
flowering.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:59.920
Timing is critical.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:04.720
Bloom too early and frost can kill a plant.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:11.720
Too late and it can be swamped by the growth of its rivals.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.920
The first sin flower are bulbs.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:31.480
The beds in front of Kew's farmhouse are transformed as they burst into colour.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.600
Then trees join the race.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:43.560
Cherries.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:51.720
And magnolias.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:12:06.080
The flowers last just long enough to attract pollinators and then they will fall.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:18.580
Meanwhile in the woods, bluebells begin to appear.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:22.920
They must grow fast if they are to collect their share of the spring sunshine before

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:45.000
the canopy develops overhead and cuts it off from them.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:51.560
But in some parts of the world, spring and summer may last only a few weeks.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:59.640
And the plants that live there have to complete their annual activities very swiftly.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:05.920
Kew makes special arrangements for them.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.760
This is the garden's newest glass house.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:24.600
It's the alpine house.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:33.720
Inside grow plants from the mountains where spring and summer are not only brief, but

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:38.360
bitter.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:43.840
This house has been built to replicate alpine conditions and it does it with this strange

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:44.840
shape.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:51.000
Deep below ground there's a labyrinth of concrete passages where the air is very cold.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:56.800
And as the temperature up here warms, so that cold air is drawn up through vents and flows

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.800
over the plants at ground level.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:05.040
And then as it warms till further, it rises and escapes through vents at the top.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:15.440
And when the sun is really strong, it's got a further trick up its sleeve.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:20.080
It's usually the melting snow that stimulates an alpine plant to bloom.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:28.840
But here technology creates such congenial conditions that alpines flower without it.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:43.680
The alpine allium, Rosacea, a Himalayan plant which is closely related to ginger.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:57.640
And Semper vivum, a plant so hardy it can root in tiny cracks between the rocks.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:03.280
Alpine plants, it's true, are nearly all tiny, but they have the beauty and fascination of

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:06.400
jewels.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:14.240
However, flowers did not evolve to please our eyes.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.920
Their function is to please insects.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:25.800
Nobody can be exactly sure how insects view the world, but it's certainly rather different

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:27.920
from the way we do.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:35.280
We can see a part of the spectrum from red, which is long wavelength light, through orange

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:42.360
and yellow and green and blue, to violet, which is short wavelength light.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:46.760
But insects can see even shorter wavelengths still.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.480
They can see ultraviolet.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:56.680
And we can use special cameras to reveal just what information that sensitivity to ultraviolet

00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:02.040
light reveals.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:13.480
We can move from how we see the world to how insects might see it.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:25.280
By looking at flowers in this way, we can begin to understand their true purpose.

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:32.480
This flower, to our eyes, seems to have uniformly plain petals.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:39.360
But the insect looking at it in ultraviolet, each petal has a white tip.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:43.200
So there's a circle of white.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:17:05.480
Drawing attention to the bull's eye, which is there, where the insect can find pollen.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:12.120
This flower, which to our eyes appears to be plain blue, in ultraviolet light has white

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:21.120
petals with lines running down it all the way around, pointing towards the center.

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:27.200
And this is the common foxglove, with, to our eyes, nothing more than a few random markings

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:30.080
on its throat.

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:34.640
But to an insect, there are more.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:40.480
These white lines probably act as landing lights, which guide it to the nectar.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:46.480
The most important structures are often the most vividly colored.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:52.600
The nectar is glow brightly.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:59.320
As does the pollen.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:05.200
The four o'clock flower blooms at dusk and has fluorescent pollen that attracts night

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:11.920
flying moths.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:21.240
This sensitivity to ultraviolet is just one way in which plants communicate with insects.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:26.080
Plants don't rely solely on color to attract their insect pollinators.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.520
They also produce smells.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:35.920
In fact, as I stand here, I'm surrounded by a swirling vortex of perfumes of many kinds.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:43.320
But unhappily, the human nostrils can only detect about 5% of them.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:55.080
Insects do very much better.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:19:06.600
We can imagine microscopic droplets of these volatile oils suspended in the air.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:12.600
Many insects have antennae that are extraordinarily sensitive to them.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:20.320
Some can detect a concentration of just a few parts per billion.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:33.640
As a consequence, insects can smell a flower from as much as a mile away.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:37.920
But messages can also travel in the other direction.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:41.760
Some insects can communicate with plants.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:48.760
They do it with sound.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:53.760
Some flowers are extremely fussy about their pollinators.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.960
This is Gustavia from the Amazon rainforest.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:01.840
Here are its flower buds.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.720
Each one will only open for a few hours.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:10.600
And in that time, it has to be visited by a particular kind of bee that buzzes with

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.600
a particular musical note.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:17.120
Well, of course, there aren't bees like that flying around here in London.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:22.720
But we have ways of deceiving Gustavia with a tuning fork.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:33.400
All we have to do now is to wait for the flowers to open.

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:51.200
A tuning fork that resonates at exactly the pitch of Gustavia's bee will cause the stamens

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.200
to vibrate.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:21:02.640
The motion releases pollen that would otherwise remain locked fast in the flower.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:08.640
The bee gains because it has exclusive access to a nutritious food source and therefore favors

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:12.720
it.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:17.240
And the plant has a courier who is almost certain to deliver the pollen to the right

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:21.840
address.

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:26.880
We've highlighted Gustavia's pollen cloud because the grains are so fine they're impossible

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:33.400
to see with the naked eye.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:50.200
But there is a way to examine even the tiniest pollen grain with an electron microscope.

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:56.420
The colors are artificial, but these pollen grains are minute.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:22:04.920
This is cedar pollen magnified 7,000 times.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:11.400
Each grain contains a tiny bundle of the particular DNA that will fertilize a flower produced

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:17.720
by another individual of the same species.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:22.760
But it has to get to such a flower.

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:28.280
Some plants use animals as couriers.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:34.640
This mountain ebony pollen is sticky and clings to the fur of bats.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:40.440
Eelgrass pollen carries a bundle of filaments that will suspend the grain in water at just

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:53.320
the depth at which the plant's flowers bloom.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:59.080
The wind-dispersed pollen grains produced by pines drift through the air with the help

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:05.000
of tiny air sacks.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:09.920
Looking at the grains through a microscope reveals how astonishingly complex their shapes

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.160
can be.

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:17.960
Each is unique to one particular species of plant.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:22.760
Only grains of that shape and the right DNA will fertilize the flowers of the species

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:30.480
that produced it.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:36.760
A pollen grain when it arrives on such a flower doesn't swim like the sperm of an animal.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:44.560
Instead, as this illustration shows, it produces a tube which goes down into the ovary at

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:54.280
the very center of the flower.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:24:01.640
With each new season, new worlds reveal themselves.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:23.200
In late spring, the longer days and stronger sunlight cue the emergence of leaves.

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:36.440
The gardens are transformed.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:52.080
Each plant arranges its leaves so that there is the minimum of overlap between them.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:57.280
They grow to fill all the available space, ensuring that every ray of light is harnessed

00:24:57.280 --> 00:25:12.160
by the green chlorophyll inside them.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:17.960
Up here in this walkway through the treetops, you can see the process as it happens.

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:22.720
In just a few weeks, the trees clothe themselves in green.

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:26.520
The amount they produce of leaves and shoots is extraordinary.

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:35.480
In just one acre, it can weigh four tons.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:42.720
During the long warm days of summer, leaves of all shapes and sizes grow in great abundance.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:50.000
And it's not just plants that depend on them.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:56.400
The rich foliage provides hidden habitats for whole communities of tiny insect herbivores

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:14.160
and their predators.

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:21.480
For many, leaves are food.

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:27.000
Blackfly get what they need by stabbing their needle-like mouthparts into the veins of leaves

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:30.000
and stems and extracting the sap.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.800
They don't even need to suck.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:42.400
The pressure inside the plant is enough to squirt the sap into their stomachs.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:46.100
Mealybugs are also sap drinkers.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:51.720
They produce a waxy powder from their skin, which most predators dislike, so that they're

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:59.600
able to drink unmolested out in the open.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:11.600
Snails rip through the vegetation, rasping off mouthfuls with a long, file-like tongue.

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:17.400
A single snail can consume a fifth of the weight of its body, shell and all, in a single

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:26.680
day.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:32.600
So insects and other small creatures, some helpful, some harmful, flourish through the

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:36.360
summer.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:46.400
This is the time when most of them reproduce, and they do so with staggering speed.

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:52.440
Female aphids mate and lay eggs like other insects, but they also produce clones, babies

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:57.040
that hatch from unfertilized eggs.

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:03.080
And the clones themselves, even before they leave a female's body, are already pregnant

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:10.560
with other clones.

00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:21.400
Such telescope generations enable aphids to infest a whole plant in a matter of hours.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:30.120
But aphids are themselves food for others, and assemblies like these don't go unnoticed

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:34.840
by other insects.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:43.640
Among the most voracious are the many kinds of ladybird and their larvae.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:49.840
This is the young of a ladybird called Cryptolamus, and it eats virtually nothing but mealybugs

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:52.560
and aphids.

00:28:52.560 --> 00:29:01.200
Even young and as an adult.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:17.200
This is the fast-moving young of a hoverfly.

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:24.880
This larva of a lacewing is totally blind, but it doesn't need to see.

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:32.580
It has an acute sense of smell, and is especially sensitive to the pheromones produced by aphids.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:38.400
It attacks its prey by impaling them on a large hook in its mouth, and then sucking

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:42.840
them dry.

00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:47.840
And it has an unquenchable appetite.

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:55.280
It will eat up to 600 aphids before it's adult.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:59.840
Most predators aren't fussy about the plants they live on, as long as there is prey to

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:03.720
hunt.

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:09.720
But one predator has formed a special partnership with a plant that looks rather like the insect-eating

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:13.080
sundew.

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:20.960
This plant, which is called Ruridula, also catches insects which get stuck to these sticky

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:25.960
hairs on its leaves.

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:36.840
But oddly enough, Ruridula can't digest insect bodies.

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:43.880
Instead, it gets help from one particular kind of insect called a capsid bug, which

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:49.400
lives nowhere else but on Ruridula.

00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:56.200
And the capsid bug runs around on these leaves without getting stuck, because its body is

00:30:56.200 --> 00:31:02.600
coated with a non-stick substance.

00:31:02.600 --> 00:31:11.520
And the capsid bug goes and feeds on the bodies of the insects that Ruridula has caught.

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:19.160
And when an insect lands and is caught, the capsid bug runs across, sticks its mouth part

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:33.960
into the insect's body and sucks it dry.

00:31:49.160 --> 00:32:04.120
Then it produces droppings which fall to the ground, and those can feed Ruridula.

00:32:04.120 --> 00:32:11.600
The carefully controlled conditions inside Kew's glass houses certainly suit plants,

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:17.320
but equally they suit insects.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:23.740
So something has to be done to keep pests in check.

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:29.120
One way is to introduce predators.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:36.440
Asian water dragons.

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:51.360
They exist on a diet of mealworms and cockroaches.

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:56.240
They quickly learn that the daily hosing down will drive the cockroaches out of the cracks

00:32:56.240 --> 00:33:10.800
where they hide during the day.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:36.880
There are also more subtle ways of controlling pests that visitors seldom notice.

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:49.120
These cards have been coated with microscopic eggs of a wasp.

00:33:49.120 --> 00:34:05.520
When they hatch, the young wasps go off in search of their favorite prey, aphids.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:09.920
This one has discovered aphid pupae.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:16.320
It selects its target and injects it with an egg of its own.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:22.680
When the larva hatches within its host, it will eat it alive, just as others have done

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:27.320
before it.

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:33.640
These are the dried out husks of victims.

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:46.760
Dried mummies.

00:34:46.760 --> 00:34:55.400
Kew is starting its next great seasonal transformation.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:35:02.000
Autumn.

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:06.360
Plants growing outside without the protection of the glass houses must get ready for the

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:10.120
bad conditions that are coming.

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:13.360
Trees prepare to lose their leaves.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:19.760
The green chlorophyll inside them begins to break down to be reabsorbed.

00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:34.960
The bright colors are byproducts of the process.

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:46.400
As the leaves fall, a new world reveals itself from beneath the soil.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:57.720
Fungi.

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:05.000
Fungi can't photosynthesize because unlike plants, they have no chlorophyll.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:10.760
In fact, they're more closely related to animals and are made of chitin, the material

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:19.520
insects use for their skeletons.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:22.640
These are the fruiting bodies of a fungus.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:28.560
Their function is to produce dust-like spores which are then blown away through the woodland

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:30.960
to grow elsewhere.

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:34.840
But these are only a tiny part of the fungus.

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:38.440
Most of the body of the fungus is beneath the ground.

00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:45.920
A tangle of tiny threads which extend for hundreds of yards through the forest.

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:52.520
And we're now beginning to realize that those threads are essential for the growth and health

00:36:52.520 --> 00:37:06.360
of many of the woodland plants.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:15.240
The length of these threads is almost unbelievable.

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:21.400
One specimen in America was found to extend across nearly four square miles.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:27.680
That's an area 16 times bigger than Kew Gardens itself.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:44.600
Technically speaking, it's the largest known living organism on the planet.

00:37:44.600 --> 00:38:01.440
These fungi make a living by feeding on the dead tissues of other organisms, both plant

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:08.280
and animal.

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:14.960
They produce powerful chemicals that enable them to break down about 90% of all organic

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:21.480
matter, including leaves and wood.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:26.440
In doing this, they release the nutrients into the soil that plants need to fuel their

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:29.360
new growth in the spring.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:38.400
So fungi are essential links in the cycle of life.

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:43.920
But some fungi establish partnerships with plants while the plants are still alive.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:49.880
And they are just as important.

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:53.040
This is the lacham oak.

00:38:53.040 --> 00:39:00.360
It germinated from an acorn in the year 1762, and it's one of the oldest plants in Kew.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:03.760
Its roots are covered with a fungus.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:05.800
But that's not an affliction.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:10.280
That's the reason why this tree was able to live for so long.

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:14.120
Because the fungus can do something that the oak tree can't.

00:39:14.120 --> 00:39:19.240
It can extract nitrogen directly from the soil, and then the oak tree collects it from

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:20.240
the fungus.

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:26.680
And in return, the fungus takes sugars from the sap in the roots of the oak tree.

00:39:26.680 --> 00:39:30.960
So it's a mutually convenient arrangement, a symbiotic relationship.

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:39.480
In fact, we now know that around 90% of the species of plants on the earth depend upon

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:43.200
fungi one way or another.

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:47.840
Kew cares for fungi just as it does for plants.

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:56.760
A special underground world has been created for them.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:40:04.600
Here thousands of different species are preserved in boxes.

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:06.800
This is the fungarium.

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:13.240
There are more specimens of fungi here than anywhere else in the world, one and a quarter

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:14.960
million of them.

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:22.560
And scientists come from all over the world here to Kew in order to study them.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:27.120
The fungarium contains specimens that Kew has collected throughout the course of its

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:30.160
400-year history.

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:36.720
They're stored for their potential value in science and medicine.

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:41.360
Perhaps the most famous is this one.

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:47.960
This is penicillium, a mold from which we get penicillin.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:53.280
There's another which is able to digest oil, and scientists are working to see whether

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:57.440
it could be useful in cleaning up oil spills.

00:40:57.440 --> 00:41:01.560
But fungi can also be very sinister.

00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:07.040
This caterpillar has a fungus growing from its head.

00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:14.280
It's a species of cordyceps, a tropical fungus that has developed a gruesome power.

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:19.080
They can infect the brain of an animal.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:26.840
One infects ants and causes an ant to climb up a grass stem, clamp its jaws on the top,

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:34.200
and there, high up on the plant, the fungus kills it.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:44.920
A long, fruiting body then bursts out of the ant's brain.

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:50.360
This elaborate behavior enables the fungus to rise high above the ground and shower its

00:41:50.360 --> 00:42:00.380
spores over great distances, and so reach new victims.

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:05.600
The world of plants is still full of secrets, even though we have so many different ways

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:13.760
of investigating their lives.

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:21.020
One of the most famous species has been something of a mystery until only a few years ago.

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:29.320
It may look like a tree, but in reality, this is just a single giant leaf.

00:42:29.320 --> 00:42:36.880
It's called the Titan Arum, and it's a record breaker.

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:39.560
But not because of what you see now.

00:42:39.560 --> 00:42:48.240
In a week or so, that green stem and the leaflets that grow on top will die and rot and disappear.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:54.200
But beneath the surface of the soil, there is a gigantic tuba, and it's from that that

00:42:54.200 --> 00:43:03.760
the record breaker will emerge.

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:10.520
This extraordinary event occurs just once every seven years.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:35.160
It will take two months to complete.

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:46.900
But this new growth is neither a trunk nor a leaf.

00:43:46.900 --> 00:44:03.200
It's the bud of the biggest flower in the world.

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:11.360
As it grows day after day, a huge spire, the spadix, rises from the center of the developing

00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:17.520
flower.

00:44:17.520 --> 00:44:40.200
And then one evening, as darkness falls over the forest, the giant flower opens.

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:52.160
This surely is one of the most astonishing of blooms.

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:58.520
I first saw one of these amazing flowers growing in the wild in the tropical rainforest of

00:44:58.520 --> 00:44:59.520
Sumatra.

00:44:59.520 --> 00:45:02.760
But why are they so big?

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:08.440
Well, the function of the flower, like all flowers, is to attract a pollinator.

00:45:08.440 --> 00:45:13.240
And this plant gives off the smell of rotting flesh.

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:24.480
But it does something else, something you can see with a heat-sensitive camera.

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:36.400
This remarkable device reveals something astonishing.

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:41.360
The white areas at the base of the spire are significantly hotter than the surrounding

00:45:41.360 --> 00:45:44.560
plant.

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:48.720
It's heating up.

00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:55.400
At its hottest, the spire can reach 37 degrees centigrade, the same temperature as the body

00:45:55.400 --> 00:46:02.160
of a mammal.

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:10.800
But as it warms, something else happens inside the flower at the base of the spire.

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:27.040
Hundreds of smaller structures begin to produce stringy pollen.

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:36.040
The titanarum is readying itself for the arrival of pollinating insects.

00:46:36.040 --> 00:46:41.880
Tiny sweat bees, and probably carrion beetles as well, are attracted by a combination of

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:46.900
the powerful smell and the heat.

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:50.680
Other flowers that smell of carrion also produce heat.

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:55.560
So it seems that what is happening is that they are mimicking the warmth of the body

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:58.480
of a recently dead animal.

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:08.760
But the hot air produced in pulses from the titanarum spire must have a different function.

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:15.960
At night, a layer of cold air, still air, forms between the forest canopy and the forest

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:16.960
floor.

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:24.440
But the spire of the titanarum, producing these pulses of warm air, pierces that barrier

00:47:24.440 --> 00:47:30.520
so that the smell of the titanarum spreads out of the top of the canopy far and wide,

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:38.140
so attracting insects, pollinating insects, from a long way away.

00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:43.720
If we could imagine such a spectacle, it would look something like smoke from a chimney discharging

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:55.400
heat into the night sky.

00:47:55.400 --> 00:48:00.560
It remains in bloom for just two days.

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:28.400
And then it closes.

00:48:28.400 --> 00:48:32.280
Plants has given us a glimpse into a hitherto unseen world.

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:36.200
However, our journey of discovery has only just started.

00:48:36.200 --> 00:48:47.760
As technology advances, so will our understanding of the hidden world of plants.

00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:58.080
The final frontier of plant discovery is in the dry zone.

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:05.640
In deserts, plants use an extraordinary range of adaptations in order to survive, by day

00:49:05.640 --> 00:49:12.640
and by night.

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:22.120
And, as we will discover, new research into plants' astonishing time capsules of life,

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:28.680
the seeds, could ensure that no plant need ever become extinct again.
